course: dictionary
lesson: 12647
title: A polite way to say unpleasant
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Displeasing is a formal word. It means not nice, or making you feel annoyed, uncomfortable or unhappy. «The smell from the old bin was displeasing to everyone in the kitchen.» The clue is that everyone holds their nose, so the smell is not nice.
explain: Authors often choose displeasing instead of unpleasant because it sounds polite and a little old-fashioned. «The noise was displeasing to the ear» is a softer way of saying the noise was horrible. It is a kind of understatement: it says less than it really means.
explain: Displeasing can also come from the verb displease, which means to make someone annoyed or unhappy. «The king was displeased when the servants arrived late.» Here the king's feelings are the clue: he was not happy about the lateness.
fact: Displeasing means not pleasant, making you feel annoyed, uncomfortable or unhappy.
fact: Displeasing is a formal word, often found in classic stories and polite speech.
fact: Displeasing is a softer way of saying unpleasant, so it is a kind of understatement.
fact: To displease someone means to make them annoyed or unhappy. «The queen was displeased by the noise.»
fact: Common phrases include displeasing to the eye, displeasing to the ear, and afraid of displeasing.
exercise: «The paint was displeasing to the eye, a muddy brown.» What does displeasing mean here? | *Not nice to look at | Happy to be seen | Very quiet | Bright and sparkly
exercise: «The king was displeased when the servants were late.» What does displeased mean in this sentence? | *Annoyed or unhappy | Very amused and laughing | Given a gift | Sleepy and tired
exercise: Mum says, «Your messy room is displeasing to me.» Which simpler word could replace displeasing? | sleepy | invisible | *unpleasant | delightful
test: «The sour smell from the old bin was displeasing to everyone in the kitchen.» What does displeasing mean? | Hidden away | Very loud | Sweet and fresh | *Not nice, making people unhappy
test: «The scratching, screeching noise was displeasing to the ear.» What is the author saying about the noise? | *It was not nice to hear | It was a soft whisper | It was a friendly song | It was too bright to see
exam: Which sentence uses displeasing correctly? | Displeasing, the sun shone bright. | We displeasing ate the bread. | *The rotten fish was displeasing to the cat's nose. | She ran displeasing across the park.
